WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
 In this section, we're going to write a web browser using Python.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.020
 So we've already got a socket, we know how to write a socket.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.400
 In the previous section, we played with the protocol

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.500
 and used Telnet to do it by hand,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.100
 and now we're going to do it in Python.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.140
 And what you're going to find is, it's not that hard.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.220
 So here we go.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.740
 So the first three lines of this program,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.700
 import socket, make the socket.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.460
 Remember, the socket isn't really got the connection.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.100
 So when you make the socket, again,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.700
 we're going to make a stream-based socket,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.820
 and it's suitable for going across the internet.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.020
 The connection, it's like ring, phone call,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.140
 connect to data.pr4e.org and port 80.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.700
 And so that basically says, extend the socket across

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.580
 and connect to a web server.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.900
 And so there's got to be a piece of software running,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.540
 and this will blow up if the software is not running.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:56.160
 So then, now we've got a phone, we've made a phone call.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.960
 Now, whether or not the remote side says hello or not

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.740
 is up to the application protocol.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.000
 And in this case, the web servers say nothing,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.380
 and they wait for you to talk first.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.580
 So we're the web browser in this case,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.260
 and so we're going to talk first.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.760
 And we know what, because we read the documentation,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.760
 we know that we're going to send get blah blah blah blah blah,

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.260
 blah blah blah blah blah, space blah blah blah blah blah,

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.700
 ht1, and then two new lines, return, return.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.360
 Remember, you had to have a blank line.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.000
 We'll talk a little bit about this encode.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.620
 It's preparing the data to go across the internet.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.300
 And then we say send it.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.540
 And so this basically takes that little string

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.580
 and sends it across the network.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.660
 And then this piece of software is waiting for it.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.060
 And then the software goes and reads a file

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:37.980
 or does some other stuff,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.260
 and then it starts sending us data back,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.220
 which we can then choose to receive.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.220
 So now I write a real simple loop.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.340
 We're going to receive these things,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.260
 512 characters at a time.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.620
 So we're going to loop through, receiving 512 each time.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.500
 And if we get zero characters,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.980
 that means it's end of the stream.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.340
 The stream is closed.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.000
 And if you look at the little example

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.380
 from the previous one, you saw connection closed.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.700
 When the connection is closed,

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.460
 we get an indication that it is

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.260
 because we ask for some data and we get zero data.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.020
 Otherwise, if there might be more data, this'll wait.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.420
 If the network is slow, you'll see,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.120
 if you do a print statement in here,

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.160
 you will see that this will pause from time to time

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.620
 on a really slow network.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.380
 If your network is fast, it'll just go blank

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.760
 and it'd be so fast it won't matter.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:22.860
 But this is how we go.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.140
 So this is basically until the entire socket,

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.100
 until the socket is closed, we're going to read this data.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.620
 And because this data's coming from the outside world,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.180
 we have to decode it before we print it.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.140
 And then when we're all done, we break out of here

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.460
 and we close the socket.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.460
 So literally, that is an entire web browser

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.660
 written in 10 lines of Python.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.860
 And again, this is why everybody loves Python.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.640
 So this is what this program will show if you run.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.780
 The get is sent.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.860
 It looks exactly like doing it by hand.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.580
 You get some headers.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.740
 Again, this is metadata that tells you something

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:04.620
 about the file.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.000
 In this case, one of the important things

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.580
 is what kind of thing is coming next.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.700
 There's always a blank line as a break

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.200
 between the headers and the actual data,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.780
 the metadata and the data.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.780
 And then here is the actual text of that romeo.txt file.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.060
 And then it's going to run this.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.340
 We're going to print data.decode.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.380
 All this is coming from the print statement.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.580
 If you were going to parse this, you have to know

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.740
 that you're going to read the headers up to a blank line.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.960
 The blank line is your indication as a software developer

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.460
 that the headers have stopped and the actual text begins,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.700
 and you know the syntax.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.580
 This actually could be a JPEG or PNG

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.100
 or some kind of image, right?

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.240
 And this data would here look like blah.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.420
 So if you type this and you change that code

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.360
 to actually talk, go retrieve a JPEG URL,

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.320
 gibberish will come out, okay?

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.420
 And so that's exactly what you will see.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:58.180
 And so now you have built a very simple web browser.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.180
 Next, I want to talk a little bit about what happens

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:06.100
 when characters transition from outside your computer,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.260
 I mean, from inside the computer in strings,

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.400
 out across these sockets to servers, and then back.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.020
 (upbeat rock music)

